  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "${\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({2}^{y+1}{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we evaluate the inner integral by integrating with respect to $x$, treating $y$ as a constant: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}({2}^{y+1}{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{2}^{y+1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{2}^{y+1}{[\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{2}^{y+1}(\\frac{{1}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{0}^{3}}{3})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{{2}^{y+1}}{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we integrate with respect to $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{{2}^{y+1}}{3}\\,\\text{d}y & ={[\\frac{{2}^{y+1}}{3\\operatorname{ln}\u20612}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{2+1}}{3\\operatorname{ln}\u20612}-\\frac{{2}^{1+1}}{3\\operatorname{ln}\u20612} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-4}{3\\operatorname{ln}\u20612} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3\\operatorname{ln}\u20612}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
